\begin{abstract}
We present QD--NESP v4.2, a unified quantum-dynamic simulation architecture that integrates
microscopic lattice Hamiltonians, non-Hermitian information-dissipation channels, and
topological invariants into a single computational framework. Using Löwdin partitioning,
the full multi-orbital Hamiltonian is reduced to an effective edge-state operator
$\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{eff}}$, enabling stable macroscopic field evolution under extreme
non-equilibrium conditions.

A non-Hermitian propagator,
\[
U(t) = \mathcal{T}\exp\left[-i\!\int H_{\mathrm{eff}}(\tau)\,d\tau 
      - \int \Gamma(\tau)\,d\tau\right],
\]
captures both coherent quantum rotation and dissipative information loss. Open-system
fluctuations are modeled via a Lindblad--Kupriyanov master equation with defect-induced
jump operators $L_k$.

Stress Regime ST--02 (Topological Defect Saturation) yields a calibrated stability
inequality,
\[
\sigma_{\min}(\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{eff}})
> \rho_d\,\frac{\Delta E}{\|\mathcal{H}_{\mathrm{eff}}\|}
+ \mathrm{Tr}(\Gamma_{\max}),
\]
which defines the precise boundary between protected topological transport and
defect-driven metallic collapse. Using empirical values from QD--NESP v4.2, we obtain
$\mathrm{Tr}(\Gamma_{\max}) \approx 0.0935\,\mathrm{eV}$.

These results demonstrate a physically consistent, numerically stable, and
topologically grounded quantum-network engine capable of operating at extreme
stress thresholds.
\end{abstract}